From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 01:02:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvk: indirect draw records through the pushbuffer
 (NVK_INDIRECT_PUSH)

CS2 on 405.8 (1920x1080 windowed, 2x MSAA) spends 548 of 998 GPU ms/s in
a depth prepass of about 80 draw calls, 77 of them vkCmdDrawIndexedIndirect
with 2.6 draws each: 8.6 ms per pass, 30-45 us per indirect draw, while
the direct draws of the same frame cost a few us per pass.  Batching the
MME reads (NVK_MDI_BATCH, 0024) changed nothing, and making host-visible
and descriptor memory GPU-uncached (NVK_RM_SYSMEM_CACHED=0) made the pass
4.6 ms: the cost is in the per-draw memory reads, not in the batching.

On Turing+ every indirect draw is an MME_DMA_READ_FIFOED of its record
that the front end waits for before it can draw.  Before Turing NVK
instead points a pushbuffer segment at the records and lets the host
fetch them as the macro's inline data.  NVK_INDIRECT_PUSH (default on;
=0 restores the MME reads) does that on Turing+ too:
vkCmdDraw[Indexed]Indirect calls new NVK_MME_DRAW[_INDEXED]_INDIRECT_INLINE
macros whose records follow the parameters in the pushbuffer, from a
no-prefetch GPFIFO entry (SYNC_WAIT, as nvk_cmd_buffer_push_indirect does
for pre-Turing) so the host fetches them only after the work that may
have written them.  One wait per call instead of one memory round trip
per draw; padding of larger strides is dropped and gl_DrawID continues
across the segments a long multi-draw is split into.  Indirect count
draws keep the MME reads (the count is only known on the GPU).

Also:
- NVK_PASS_PROFILE=2 reported its counters at pipeline location ALL,
  which faulted the channel in CS2.  Each counter is now reported at its
  own location with flush_disable, as NVK's pipeline statistics queries
  do; the ZCULL counters only with NVK_PASS_PROFILE=3, at the ZCULL
  location.
- NVK_RM_SYSMEM_CACHED=desc or =app makes only descriptor memory or only
  coherent host-visible memory GPU-cacheable, to tell which of the two
  the GPU reads on the slow path (1 and 0 unchanged).

Checked in the MME simulator: the inline macro's method stream equals the
per-record DMA loop's for strides of 4-16 dwords, 1-1000 draws and the
segment splits.  Not measured on hardware yet.
